# Authoring an agent

An [`agent`](/language/kinds-reference) is a **sandboxed function**: a server-side unit
whose whole definition lives in one block. Where a [`server`](/language/placement-and-languages)
unit runs code you wrote, an agent reads a goal and acts — it reasons, runs tools, reads
the results, and loops until it's done. You define *what it knows, what it can do, what it
may touch, and when it stops* — and nothing outside the block is needed.

## The four fields

```
agent :analyst do
  prompt """
  You are a terse data analyst. COMPUTE every number with bash against /work — never
  guess one. When you have the answer, reply in exactly the requested format.
  """

  tools coreutils, web              # capabilities — the kits it may run
  grant fs                          # permissions — the host powers it may use
  limit turns: 20, timeout: 120_000 # guardrails — when it must stop
end
```

- **`prompt`** — the instructions, a `"""heredoc"""` (the encapsulation; nothing escapes,
  so it can contain quotes, code, markdown). This is the only *prose* field.
- **`tools`** — its capabilities: the [kits](/tooling/toolkits) it's allowed to run in
  `bash` (`coreutils`, `web`, any `toolkit` you authored). Declared, not global.
- **`grant`** — its permissions: the host powers (`fs`, `net`, `exec`, …) it may use. This
  is the sandbox boundary, written into the definition. → [Grants & capabilities](/capabilities/grants-and-capabilities)
- **`limit`** — guardrails (`turns`, `timeout`) so an autonomous run can't run away.

`tools`/`grant`/`limit` are composable Elixir — a value, a list, or a reference to
something defined elsewhere. **Shorthand:** if all you need is a prompt, write the prose
straight in the body — `agent :x do …instructions… end` — and the runtime treats the whole
body as the prompt. The four-field form is the canonical one; the shorthand is for a quick
brain with default capabilities.

## How it's contained

Everything the agent does, it does through `bash` — and `bash` is not a host shell. Each
command is a **wasm kit run in wasmtime against a `/work` virtual filesystem**, so the
agent reads and writes only inside `/work` and runs only the kits in its `tools`. It can't
reach the host filesystem, spawn host processes, or open a raw socket; web access is a
brokered, SSRF-gated capability. `grant` sets the boundary, `limit` sets the stop — the
blast radius of an autonomous agent is its own sandbox. → [Sandboxing & the Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock)

## Running it

`agent` is a server-side kind — it runs on the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus),
on the BEAM, where the model and tools live. The runtime drives the loop for you: call the
model with `bash` available → run any command in the sandbox → feed the output back →
repeat → stop on a final answer or a `limit`. To run one from your own code (seeding files,
capturing edits), call `Nexus.Agent.run/1` with the **same fields** as the block:

```
Nexus.Agent.run(
  prompt: "You are a terse data analyst …",
  tools:  [:coreutils, :web],
  grant:  [:fs],
  task:   "How many unique users are in users.csv?",
  seed:   %{"users.csv" => csv}
)
# => {:ok, %{answer: "…", vfs_files: %{…}}}
```

Declare a reusable agent with the block; define one inline here for a one-off — the field
names mean the same thing in both. See it built end to end in [A coding agent](/examples/coding-agent)
and fanned out in [Agents at scale](/examples/agents-at-scale).

## Declared *and* enforced

The agent's powers aren't just documentation — they're enforced at runtime. `tools` scopes
what its `bash` may run: a command whose kit isn't in `tools` is refused. `grant` gates the
host powers — a web command with no `web`/`net` grant is refused. `limit` stops a runaway
run at its turn or wall-clock budget. So you review an agent by reading the block — which
kits, which host powers — with no hidden prompt, tool registry, or ambient access, and the
[capability audit](/capabilities/grants-and-capabilities) checks it just like a `sandbox`.
To add a power, write a [toolkit](/tooling/toolkits) and list it in `tools`.

## Reacting to a run

Tag an agent `#event` and each run emits an event onto the bus, carrying the agent's other
`#tags`. A [`hook`](/language/kinds-reference) then matches those events and fires effects —
notify, run another agent, or kick off a [`flow`](/language/kinds-reference). That's the
reactive loop: an agent *acts*, a hook *reacts*, a flow *sequences* — all declared, all in
the workbook.
